import MassGap.StrongArm  -- THE STRONG-COUPLING ARM of the substrate hypothesis. substrate_of_contact_relative_decay wants rho(d) <= C*rho(0)/circLag(d)^4 at EVERY aperture and EVERY coupling; contact_relative_on_strong_arm produces one (C, m0) valid on a DERIVED coupling interval [0, b], with b the existential of core_rate_lt_one_of_small_hypercubic (no numeral, nothing chosen) and m0 = 1, so no lag but the contact term is excluded. coreRate_mono_beta / corePrefactor_mono_beta / coreConst_mono_beta are the coupling monotonicities that let the value at the endpoint serve the whole closed interval -- the tree had monotonicity in the DEGREE only. exists_geom_quartic_bound shows a geometric sequence dominates a quartic OUTRIGHT rather than eventually, which is what removes the cut. wilsonCorrAt_eq_corrClay checks the identification by rfl. THE RESULT IS CONDITIONAL AND THE CONDITION IS THE POINT: ContactFloor b, one positive lower bound on rho(0) uniform in the APERTURE, IS NOW PROVED in MassGap.ContactFloor (contactFloor_holds, foundational only), so contact_relative_unconditional carries NOTHING. The obstacle was that every quantitative Gibbs-vs-Haar bound in the tree carried an exponent proportional to the PLAQUETTE COUNT; the way past it is that the exponent only has to count the plaquettes SHARING A LINK with the one being read, which touchDeg_bd_le caps at 16*dim = 64 with no extent, giving e^{-128*beta} at every aperture. The remaining constant is pure Haar: at beta = 0 the measure IS Haar, so the contact value is one SU(3) number, haarSecond - haarMean^2, identical at every extent >= 2, positive by the non-constructive corrClay_zero_pos -- and non-constructive is harmless there because it is a universal constant carrying no aperture and no coupling. This still does NOT discharge the substrate hypothesis, which quantifies over EVERY coupling: it converts open-on-the-whole-coupling-line into open-on-[b, infinity). And it cannot be pushed further -- the floor deg